    "params": {
        "video_subject": "7 · Would you take less money to be richer?",
        "video_script": "Number seven: would you take less money to be richer? Researchers at Harvard asked students and staff to imagine two worlds, where prices are the same. In world A, you earn fifty thousand dollars a year, and everyone else earns twenty-five thousand. In world B, you earn one hundred thousand a year, and everyone else earns two hundred thousand. Which world do you choose? Take a second. In world B you'd have twice the money, and it buys exactly the same things. And yet about half the people chose world A. Half the money, as long as they were richer than everyone around them. And these were people at a school of public health: educated, thoughtful, answering a calm survey with no one watching. That's the engine behind everything in this video. Your brain doesn't measure wealth in dollars. It measures it in comparison. The wine, the cookies, the logo, the quiet bag, the red sneakers, even the fake sunglasses: they're all about where you stand next to other people. And the same survey found something telling: for holidays, most people chose more time off for themselves, even if others got more. Comparison hits hardest on the things other people can see. Try this today: the next time you feel poor, check who you're comparing yourself to. Compare with your own life a year ago instead. That's the one comparison that tells you if you're actually getting richer. If you want to see how the same brain loses money in everyday spending, watch this next: why your brain is bad with money.",
